    "q-81231": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81231",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}+4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})+4(\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{16}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$",
